"{\"eval_set_result_id\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640896.5454094\",\"eval_set_result_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent_Evaluation_test_1_1763640896.5454094\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_case_results\":[{\"eval_set_file\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_set_id\":\"Evaluation_test_1\",\"eval_id\":\"test-1\",\"final_eval_status\":2,\"eval_metric_results\":null,\"overall_eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5721518987341772,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}],\"eval_metric_result_per_invocation\":[{\"actual_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-609c22d2-986f-4d55-b284-f54138831c29\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: ItineraryBuilderAgent has processed the destination research and formulated a comprehensive 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto.\\n\\nThis itinerary balances iconic sightseeing with immersive cultural activities, ensuring efficient travel by grouping attractions geographically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Immersion\\n\\n**Focus:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Recommended Travel Season:** Spring (April/May) or Autumn (October/November)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor the best balance of accessibility, atmosphere, and proximity to dining:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama/Gion Area (Recommended for Culture):** Closest to many iconic temples, traditional streets, and evening atmosphere. Excellent for soaking in the old-Kyoto vibe.\\n2.  **Kawaramachi/Downtown Area:** Central hub, best for transit links (buses/subway) and modern dining variety.\\n3.  **Central Kyoto (Near Kyoto Station):** Best for day trips and ease of arrival/departure, but slightly less charming for evening ambiance.\\n\\n## Estimated Daily Budget Breakdown (Excluding Accommodation)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range estimate for one person, assuming IC card usage for transit and a mix of casual and slightly nicer dinners.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Daily Cost (USD, Approx.) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Entry Fees** | \u00a52,500 - \u00a54,000 | $17 - $27 | Based on 3-4 temple entries per day. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 - \u00a510,000 | $40 - $67 | Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Snacks. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | $7 - $10 | Daily bus/subway use (using IC Card). |\\n| **Cultural Activity** | Varies (Budgeted separately below) | - | Tea Ceremony, special workshops. |\\n| **TOTAL (Excl. Activity/Hotels)** | **\u00a59,500 - \u00a515,500** | **$64 - $104** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed 7-Day Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and The Spiritual Heart of Eastern Kyoto (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive in Kyoto, check into accommodation, purchase IC Card (e.g., ICOCA). | 3 hours | Quick breakfast near the station or hotel. | Familiarize yourself with the local bus network immediately. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** Explore the main hall and wooden veranda. | 2 hours | Lunch near Kiyomizu-dera (Try **Yuba**\u2014tofu skin specialties). | Be prepared for crowds; observe purification rituals at the Otowa Waterfall. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk the historic **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets. | 1.5 hours | Stop for Matcha soft serve or Japanese sweets. | Excellent for photography; watch for tourists photographing Maiko/Geiko respectfully. |\\n| **Evening** | Explore **Gion District** and **Hanamikoji Street**. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Traditional *Kaiseki* (if budget allows) or affordable *Okonomiyaki* in the Pontocho Alley area. | Keep noise levels low in Gion; do not approach or stop Geiko/Maiko for photos. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kiyomizu-dera Entry: \u00a5400 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Golden Beauty and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel North. **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. | 1.5 hours | Grab a quick *Onigiri* (rice ball) for breakfast on the go. | Arrive right at opening (9:00 AM) to experience the reflection on the pond with fewer crowds. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | Lunch near the temple area (simple noodle shop recommended). | Sit quietly and observe the rock garden; avoid touching the stones. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former Shogun\u2019s residence, focusing on the Nightingale Floors. | 2 hours | Tea/Coffee break. | Listen for the squeaking \\\"nightingale floors\\\" designed as security alarms. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore Nishiki Market area for diverse street food or casual *Izakaya* dining. | Nishiki Market usually closes early, so focus on nearby restaurants. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kinkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500, Ryoan-ji Entry: \u00a5600, Nijo Castle Entry: \u00a51,040 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Immersion in Tradition \u2013 Tea Ceremony and Craft\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Cultural Workshop:** Participate in a formal **Tea Ceremony (Chanoyu)**. | 2 hours | Light breakfast before the ceremony. | **Crucial Cultural Deep Dive:** Book in advance. Focus on the precise movements and philosophy. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Explore the dry sand garden (\\\"Sea of Silver Sand\\\"). | 1.5 hours | Lunch near Ginkaku-ji (Look for shops selling traditional Kyoto sweets). | A much calmer contrast to Kinkaku-ji; enjoy the moss garden paths. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A reflective walk along the canal. | 1.5 hours | Stop at local craft shops along the path for browsing. | Especially beautiful during Sakura or autumn foliage season. |\\n| **Evening** | Free time/Relaxation. | Flexible | **Dinner:** Try Kyoto-style *Obanzai* (home-style local cooking). | Use this evening to relax after two intense days of sightseeing. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Ginkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000 - \u00a56,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Thousand Gates and Southern Majesty (Fushimi & Nara Option)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Hike through the thousands of red *torii* gates. | 3 - 4 hours | Early light breakfast. Purchase local street snacks (like *Inari Sushi*) near the base. | Go as early as possible (before 8 AM) to experience the gates without massive crowds. You do not need to hike to the very top. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near Fushimi Inari Station. | 1 hour | Lunch at a local spot specializing in eel or simple udon. | |\\n| **Afternoon (Option A)**| **Day Trip to Nara:** Visit Todai-ji Temple (Giant Buddha) and interact with the friendly wild deer. | 5 hours (Including travel) | Street snacks in Nara (e.g., *mochi* pounding demonstration). | Nara is 45 mins by JR train from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon (Option B)**| **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d:** Visit the hall containing 1,001 statues of Kannon. | 1.5 hours | Relaxing afternoon back in Kyoto. | Photography is strictly forbidden inside the hall; marvel at the precision of the statues. |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner:** Explore dining options around Kyoto Station (great for variety and efficiency). | Flexible | Dinner: Ramen street in Kyoto Station or Isetan department store dining floors. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Fushimi Inari: Free. Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d: \u00a5600. Nara (Todai-ji): \u00a5600. | | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Bamboo Groves and Western Serenity (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama (JR Saga Line recommended). **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | 2 hours | Breakfast at the hotel or a bakery near Saga-Arashiyama station. | Visit the Bamboo Grove immediately upon arrival (before 9 AM) for the best experience. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple:** Explore the main temple and its extensive landscape garden. | 2 hours | Lunch near the Katsura River. Try vegetarian *shojin ryori* (monk's cuisine) if available. | A UNESCO site; the garden seamlessly integrates the surrounding mountains. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Cross the **Togetsukyo Bridge** and explore the smaller, quieter temples on the western bank (e.g., Okochi Sanso Villa). | 2.5 hours | Stop for sweets or green tea near the river. | Okochi Sanso (former actor's villa) offers spectacular views and includes tea in the entry fee. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore the dining area near your hotel or try a specialized *Tempura* restaurant. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Tenry\u016b-ji Entry: \u00a5500 (Garden only), Okochi Sanso: \u00a51,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Culture & Artistic Exploration\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Kyoto National Museum** or **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (depending on interest). | 3 hours | Breakfast/Brunch near the museum area. | Great for a slightly less temple-focused cultural day. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch. | 1 hour | Ramen or Curry. | |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Area Revisit (Optional):** Explore smaller, less-visited sub-temples (like K\u014ddai-ji) or traditional pottery shops in Kiyomizu-zaka. | 3 hours | Second chance for a traditional sweet treat. | This allows time for souvenir shopping focusing on local crafts (pottery, fans). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| **Shinto Tradition Immersion:** Visit a smaller, local shrine (e.g., Kitano Tenman-g\u016b if time permits) to observe daily rituals. | 1.5 hours | | Observe the *ema* (prayer plaques) and local customs. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner:** Splurge on a memorable Kyoto dining experience (e.g., high-quality Wagyu or specialized Sushi). | Flexible | **Dinner:** Reservation highly recommended for the farewell meal. | Reflect on the trip; purchase any last-minute gifts. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Museum Entry: Approx. \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Reflections and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Morning Meditation/Temple Visit:** Revisit a favorite quiet spot (like a smaller Zen garden) or visit a local market for breakfast. | 2 hours | A final, classic Japanese breakfast set (grilled fish, rice, miso soup). | Use this time for quiet reflection before the busy travel day. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Final packing and check-out. | 1.5 hours | | Ensure all transportation tickets for onward travel are ready. |\\n| **Noon/Afternoon**| Travel to Kansai International Airport (KIX) or onward destination. | Varies | Quick and easy lunch near Kyoto Station before departure. | The JR Haruka Express is the most efficient way to KIX from Kyoto Station (approx. 75 minutes). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Major Budget Estimates (7 Days, Per Person)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (USD) | Mid-Range Estimate (USD) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (7 Nights)** | $700 (Budget Hostel/Guesthouse) | $1,200 (Mid-range Hotel/Airbnb) | Highly variable based on season. |\\n| **Food & Dining (7 Days)** | $300 ($43/day) | $550 ($78/day) | Includes one minor splurge meal. |\\n| **Activities/Entry Fees** | $100 | $150 | Covers all temple/museum entries researched above. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences (Tea Ceremony)** | $40 | $60 | |\\n| **Local Transport (IC Card Top-ups)** | $50 | $70 | Covers local travel within Kyoto city limits. |\\n| **Intercity Travel (e.g., to KIX)** | $30 | $40 | One-way estimate to major airport/station. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE (Excluding Flights)** | **$1,220** | **$2,070** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n# OPTIMIZATION TIPS (TravelOptimizerAgent)\\n\\n### Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n1.  **IC Card Power:** Use an **IC Card (ICOCA)** for transport, but be strategic about the bus. If you plan to take more than 3 bus rides in a day (which is common in Kyoto), purchase the **Kyoto City Bus All-Day Pass** (approx. \u00a5700). This saves money versus paying the flat fare (around \u00a5230 per ride) multiple times.\\n2.  **Temple Hopping Discounts:** While not universal, some temples near each other offer small reciprocal discounts, or you can utilize the **Temple and Shrine Association** pamphlets sometimes available near Kyoto Station.\\n3.  **Lunch vs. Dinner:** Many high-end restaurants (like Kaiseki or quality Sushi) offer significantly cheaper **lunch sets** (*teishoku*) than their dinner menus. Book lunch for your splurge meal on Days 1 or 6.\\n4.  **Free Attractions:** Maximize time at free sites: Fushimi Inari Shrine, the Philosopher's Path, and wandering Gion/Pontocho Alley. Even the exterior viewing of Kinkaku-ji is often the highlight.\\n5.  **Water Refills:** Buy one large reusable bottle. Kyoto has excellent tap water, saving significantly on bottled drinks.\\n\\n### Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\n1.  **Footwear is Key:** You will be removing your shoes frequently (temples, traditional restaurants, tea ceremonies) and walking miles on uneven stone paths. Pack comfortable, easy-to-slip-on/off shoes (slip-ons or low-cut sneakers are ideal).\\n2.  **Layered Clothing:** Especially if traveling in shoulder seasons (Spring/Autumn), mornings and evenings can be cool while midday is warm. Layers are essential.\\n3.  **Modest Cover-Up:** Bring a light scarf or cardigan. While major temples don't usually require covering shoulders/knees, it is respectful for smaller, local shrines or during intense heat/cold.\\n4.  **Small Towel/Handkerchief:** Public restrooms often lack paper towels or dryers. Carrying a small personal hand towel is standard Japanese practice.\\n5.  **Portable Wi-Fi/SIM Card:** Essential for navigation (Google Maps is superb in Japan). Purchase a pocket Wi-Fi router or eSIM prior to arrival.\\n\\n### Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n1.  **Rainy Day Alternatives (If Visiting in Summer/Spring):**\\n    *   **Day 2 Backup:** Dedicate more time to **Nijo Castle** (mostly indoors/covered walkways).\\n    *   **Day 6 Backup:** Replace museum time with an **Indoor Activity** like a Japanese cooking class focused on *washoku* or an extended visit to the large department store basement food halls (*depachika*) near Kyoto Station.\\n    *   **Tea Ceremony Flexibility:** If it rains on Day 3, consider booking a tea ceremony that includes an indoor garden viewing or moves it to Day 6.\\n2.  **Crowd Control Backup (Especially Arashiyama/Fushimi Inari):** If Fushimi Inari or Arashiyama are overwhelmingly crowded, **reverse the schedule**. Go to Arashiyama late in the afternoon (4 PM onwards) when most day-trippers have left, or visit Fushimi Inari just before sunset.\\n3.  **Transportation Disruption:** If buses are severely delayed (common during peak tourist events), utilize the **subway** for North-South routes (Karasuma line) and **taxis** for short hops between closely grouped temples (e.g., Kinkaku-ji to Ryoan-ji).\\n\\n### Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Essential for real-time public transit schedules (bus routes are highly accurate here).\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan/Navitime):** Excellent secondary apps for detailed train/shinkansen schedules, especially useful if taking the optional Nara trip.\\n3.  **Google Translate (Download Japanese Offline Pack):** Useful for reading complex signs or ordering food where English menus are unavailable.\\n4.  **Wego/Booking.com:** For last-minute accommodation checks, though booking ahead is highly recommended for peak season.\\n5.  **Restaurant Apps (e.g., Tabelog/Gurunavi):** Use these to find highly-rated local spots, filtering by area (e.g., Pontocho, Kawaramachi). *Note: Tabelog ratings can be very strict.*\\n\\n### Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| DO | DON'T |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **Bowing:** A slight nod of the head is appreciated when thanking service staff. | **Tipping:** Never leave cash on the table; it may be confusing or slightly insulting. |\\n| **Noise Control:** Keep conversations quiet on buses, trains, and especially inside temple grounds. | **Eat/Drink While Walking:** Outside of festival stalls or the immediate areas around Fushimi Inari, eating while moving is frowned upon. |\\n| **Prepare Cash:** Many smaller temples, artisan shops, and buses prefer or require cash (Yen). | **Point Directly:** Avoid pointing at people (especially Maiko/Geiko) or religious icons. Gesture subtly with an open hand. |\\n| **Accepting Items:** Receive business cards or gifts with both hands if offered. | **Enter Private Property:** Never step over ropes or walk onto grass/moss gardens unless explicitly permitted. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"invocation_events\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640874.073748,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":{\"agent_details\":{\"DestinationResearchAgent\":{\"name\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a travel researcher. You will be given a destination and travel preferences, and you will research:\\n    - Best time to visit and weather patterns\\n    - Top attractions and must-see locations\\n    - Local culture, customs, and etiquette tips\\n    - Transportation options within the destination\\n    - Safety considerations and travel requirements\\n    Provide comprehensive destination insights for trip planning.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"DestinationResearchAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that researches travel destinations and gathers essential information\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[{\"function_declarations\":null,\"retrieval\":null,\"google_search_retrieval\":null,\"computer_use\":null,\"file_search\":null,\"code_execution\":null,\"enterprise_web_search\":null,\"google_maps\":null,\"google_search\":{\"exclude_domains\":null,\"blocking_confidence\":null,\"time_range_filter\":null},\"url_context\":null}]},\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\":{\"name\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a professional travel planner. Using the research from \\\"destination_research\\\" output, create a detailed itinerary that includes:\\n    - Day-by-day schedule with recommended activities\\n    - Suggested accommodation areas or districts\\n    - Estimated time requirements for each activity\\n    - Meal recommendations and dining suggestions\\n    - Budget estimates for major expenses\\n    Structure it logically for easy following during the trip.\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"ItineraryBuilderAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that creates structured travel itineraries with daily schedules\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]},\"TravelOptimizerAgent\":{\"name\":\"TravelOptimizerAgent\",\"instructions\":\"\\n    You are a seasoned travel consultant. Using the itinerary from \\\"travel_itinerary\\\" output, optimize it by adding:\\n    - Money-saving tips and budget alternatives\\n    - Packing recommendations specific to the destination\\n    - Backup plans for weather or unexpected situations\\n    - Local apps, websites, or resources to download\\n    - Cultural do's and don'ts for respectful travel\\n\\n    Format the final output as:\\n    \\n    ITINERARY: ItineraryBuilderAgent has processed the destination research and formulated a comprehensive 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto.\\n\\nThis itinerary balances iconic sightseeing with immersive cultural activities, ensuring efficient travel by grouping attractions geographically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Immersion\\n\\n**Focus:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Recommended Travel Season:** Spring (April/May) or Autumn (October/November)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor the best balance of accessibility, atmosphere, and proximity to dining:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama/Gion Area (Recommended for Culture):** Closest to many iconic temples, traditional streets, and evening atmosphere. Excellent for soaking in the old-Kyoto vibe.\\n2.  **Kawaramachi/Downtown Area:** Central hub, best for transit links (buses/subway) and modern dining variety.\\n3.  **Central Kyoto (Near Kyoto Station):** Best for day trips and ease of arrival/departure, but slightly less charming for evening ambiance.\\n\\n## Estimated Daily Budget Breakdown (Excluding Accommodation)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range estimate for one person, assuming IC card usage for transit and a mix of casual and slightly nicer dinners.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Daily Cost (USD, Approx.) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Entry Fees** | \u00a52,500 - \u00a54,000 | $17 - $27 | Based on 3-4 temple entries per day. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 - \u00a510,000 | $40 - $67 | Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Snacks. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | $7 - $10 | Daily bus/subway use (using IC Card). |\\n| **Cultural Activity** | Varies (Budgeted separately below) | - | Tea Ceremony, special workshops. |\\n| **TOTAL (Excl. Activity/Hotels)** | **\u00a59,500 - \u00a515,500** | **$64 - $104** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed 7-Day Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and The Spiritual Heart of Eastern Kyoto (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive in Kyoto, check into accommodation, purchase IC Card (e.g., ICOCA). | 3 hours | Quick breakfast near the station or hotel. | Familiarize yourself with the local bus network immediately. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** Explore the main hall and wooden veranda. | 2 hours | Lunch near Kiyomizu-dera (Try **Yuba**\u2014tofu skin specialties). | Be prepared for crowds; observe purification rituals at the Otowa Waterfall. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk the historic **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets. | 1.5 hours | Stop for Matcha soft serve or Japanese sweets. | Excellent for photography; watch for tourists photographing Maiko/Geiko respectfully. |\\n| **Evening** | Explore **Gion District** and **Hanamikoji Street**. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Traditional *Kaiseki* (if budget allows) or affordable *Okonomiyaki* in the Pontocho Alley area. | Keep noise levels low in Gion; do not approach or stop Geiko/Maiko for photos. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kiyomizu-dera Entry: \u00a5400 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Golden Beauty and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel North. **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. | 1.5 hours | Grab a quick *Onigiri* (rice ball) for breakfast on the go. | Arrive right at opening (9:00 AM) to experience the reflection on the pond with fewer crowds. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | Lunch near the temple area (simple noodle shop recommended). | Sit quietly and observe the rock garden; avoid touching the stones. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former Shogun\u2019s residence, focusing on the Nightingale Floors. | 2 hours | Tea/Coffee break. | Listen for the squeaking \\\"nightingale floors\\\" designed as security alarms. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore Nishiki Market area for diverse street food or casual *Izakaya* dining. | Nishiki Market usually closes early, so focus on nearby restaurants. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kinkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500, Ryoan-ji Entry: \u00a5600, Nijo Castle Entry: \u00a51,040 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Immersion in Tradition \u2013 Tea Ceremony and Craft\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Cultural Workshop:** Participate in a formal **Tea Ceremony (Chanoyu)**. | 2 hours | Light breakfast before the ceremony. | **Crucial Cultural Deep Dive:** Book in advance. Focus on the precise movements and philosophy. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Explore the dry sand garden (\\\"Sea of Silver Sand\\\"). | 1.5 hours | Lunch near Ginkaku-ji (Look for shops selling traditional Kyoto sweets). | A much calmer contrast to Kinkaku-ji; enjoy the moss garden paths. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A reflective walk along the canal. | 1.5 hours | Stop at local craft shops along the path for browsing. | Especially beautiful during Sakura or autumn foliage season. |\\n| **Evening** | Free time/Relaxation. | Flexible | **Dinner:** Try Kyoto-style *Obanzai* (home-style local cooking). | Use this evening to relax after two intense days of sightseeing. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Ginkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000 - \u00a56,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Thousand Gates and Southern Majesty (Fushimi & Nara Option)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Hike through the thousands of red *torii* gates. | 3 - 4 hours | Early light breakfast. Purchase local street snacks (like *Inari Sushi*) near the base. | Go as early as possible (before 8 AM) to experience the gates without massive crowds. You do not need to hike to the very top. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near Fushimi Inari Station. | 1 hour | Lunch at a local spot specializing in eel or simple udon. | |\\n| **Afternoon (Option A)**| **Day Trip to Nara:** Visit Todai-ji Temple (Giant Buddha) and interact with the friendly wild deer. | 5 hours (Including travel) | Street snacks in Nara (e.g., *mochi* pounding demonstration). | Nara is 45 mins by JR train from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon (Option B)**| **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d:** Visit the hall containing 1,001 statues of Kannon. | 1.5 hours | Relaxing afternoon back in Kyoto. | Photography is strictly forbidden inside the hall; marvel at the precision of the statues. |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner:** Explore dining options around Kyoto Station (great for variety and efficiency). | Flexible | Dinner: Ramen street in Kyoto Station or Isetan department store dining floors. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Fushimi Inari: Free. Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d: \u00a5600. Nara (Todai-ji): \u00a5600. | | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Bamboo Groves and Western Serenity (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama (JR Saga Line recommended). **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | 2 hours | Breakfast at the hotel or a bakery near Saga-Arashiyama station. | Visit the Bamboo Grove immediately upon arrival (before 9 AM) for the best experience. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple:** Explore the main temple and its extensive landscape garden. | 2 hours | Lunch near the Katsura River. Try vegetarian shojin ryori (monk's cuisine) if available. | A UNESCO site; the garden seamlessly integrates the surrounding mountains. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Cross the **Togetsukyo Bridge** and explore the smaller, quieter temples on the western bank (e.g., Okochi Sanso Villa). | 2.5 hours | Stop for sweets or green tea near the river. | Okochi Sanso (former actor's villa) offers spectacular views and includes tea in the entry fee. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore the dining area near your hotel or try a specialized *Tempura* restaurant. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Tenry\u016b-ji Entry: \u00a5500 (Garden only), Okochi Sanso: \u00a51,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Culture & Artistic Exploration\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Kyoto National Museum** or **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (depending on interest). | 3 hours | Breakfast/Brunch near the museum area. | Great for a slightly less temple-focused cultural day. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch. | 1 hour | Ramen or Curry. | |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Area Revisit (Optional):** Explore smaller, less-visited sub-temples (like K\u014ddai-ji) or traditional pottery shops in Kiyomizu-zaka. | 3 hours | Second chance for a traditional sweet treat. | This allows time for souvenir shopping focusing on local crafts (pottery, fans). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| **Shinto Tradition Immersion:** Visit a smaller, local shrine (e.g., Kitano Tenman-g\u016b if time permits) to observe daily rituals. | 1.5 hours | | Observe the *ema* (prayer plaques) and local customs. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner:** Splurge on a memorable Kyoto dining experience (e.g., high-quality Wagyu or specialized Sushi). | Flexible | **Dinner:** Reservation highly recommended for the farewell meal. | Reflect on the trip; purchase any last-minute gifts. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Museum Entry: Approx. \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Reflections and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Morning Meditation/Temple Visit:** Revisit a favorite quiet spot (like a smaller Zen garden) or visit a local market for breakfast. | 2 hours | A final, classic Japanese breakfast set (grilled fish, rice, miso soup). | Use this time for quiet reflection before the busy travel day. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Final packing and check-out. | 1.5 hours | | Ensure all transportation tickets for onward travel are ready. |\\n| **Noon/Afternoon**| Travel to Kansai International Airport (KIX) or onward destination. | Varies | Quick and easy lunch near Kyoto Station before departure. | The JR Haruka Express is the most efficient way to KIX from Kyoto Station (approx. 75 minutes). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Major Budget Estimates (7 Days, Per Person)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (USD) | Mid-Range Estimate (USD) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (7 Nights)** | $700 (Budget Hostel/Guesthouse) | $1,200 (Mid-range Hotel/Airbnb) | Highly variable based on season. |\\n| **Food & Dining (7 Days)** | $300 ($43/day) | $550 ($78/day) | Includes one minor splurge meal. |\\n| **Activities/Entry Fees** | $100 | $150 | Covers all temple/museum entries researched above. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences (Tea Ceremony)** | $40 | $60 | |\\n| **Local Transport (IC Card Top-ups)** | $50 | $70 | Covers local travel within Kyoto city limits. |\\n| **Intercity Travel (e.g., to KIX)** | $30 | $40 | One-way estimate to major airport/station. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE (Excluding Flights)** | **$1,220** | **$2,070** | |\\n\\n    OPTIMIZATION TIPS: [your money-saving and practical tips here]\\n\\n    TRAVEL ESSENTIALS: [packing and preparation advice here]\\n\\n    BACKUP PLANS: [alternative options and contingencies here]\\n    \\n\\nYou are an agent. Your internal name is \\\"TravelOptimizerAgent\\\". The description about you is \\\"An agent that optimizes travel plans with practical advice and alternatives\\\".\",\"tool_declarations\":[]}}}},\"expected_invocation\":{\"invocation_id\":\"e-8e149827-dcc4-4e6c-8b8c-6409e9319355\",\"user_content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"final_response\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: This is Agent **ItineraryBuilderAgent**. I will now structure the detailed 7-day cultural deep-dive itinerary for Kyoto based on the provided research framework, enhanced with practical optimization tips.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary (Optimized)\\n\\nThis itinerary focuses on minimizing travel time by grouping activities geographically, maximizing exposure to temples, deep traditions (tea/Zen), and natural beauty, while integrating essential budget, packing, and contingency planning.\\n\\n## I. Trip Overview & Logistics (Unchanged)\\n\\n*(Logistics, Budget Estimates, and Suggested Accommodation Areas remain as detailed in the original plan.)*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## II. Daily Itinerary (Optimized with Budget & Contingency Notes)\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival & Southern Icon \u2013 The Thousand Gates\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrival at KIX/ITM, transfer to Kyoto Station, check in. | Logistics | 3 hours | Grab quick *onigiri* or station bento box (*ekiben*). | **BUDGET TIP:** If flying into KIX, the **JR Haruka Express** is efficient but expensive. For savings, consider the *Limousine Bus* or the cheaper *JR Kansai Airport Rapid Service* to Osaka, then transfer to a local train to Kyoto. |\\n| **15:00** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha** Exploration. | Temple/Nature | 3.5 hours | Hike at least halfway up. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring sturdy, comfortable walking shoes\u2014the path is uneven stone/dirt. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner near accommodation. | Local Cuisine | 1.5 hours | **Dinner:** Local *Yakiniku* or ramen. | **LOCAL APP:** Download **Google Maps** (for real-time bus tracking) and **Japan Transit Planner** (Jorudan) for all intercity routes. |\\n\\n### Day 2: Eastern Majesty \u2013 History, View, and Tea\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Higashiyama District (East)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **08:00** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** visit. | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Must arrive early. | **BUDGET TIP:** Buy a Kyoto One-Day Bus Pass (\u00a5700) if you take 3+ bus rides today. |\\n| **09:30** | Walk down **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka**. | Historic Streets | 1.5 hours | Sample *yatsuhashi*. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not block the pathway for photos on these narrow streets; tourists clogging traffic leads to resentment. |\\n| **14:00** | **Traditional Tea Ceremony Experience.** | Deep Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book in advance. | **CULTURAL DO:** Arrive silently. Observe how the host handles the utensils with reverence. |\\n| **19:00** | Dinner in Gion/Pontocho Alley. | Dining | 2 hours | Pontocho Alley dining. | **BACKUP PLAN (Gion):** If Pontocho is too crowded/expensive, pivot to Kawaramachi for a wider range of *Izakaya* options. |\\n\\n### Day 3: Northern Reflection \u2013 Zen and Gold\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Northern Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **10:00** | **Kinkaku-ji** (Golden Pavilion). | Iconic Temple | 1.5 hours | Aim for morning sunlight. | **BUDGET TIP:** Kinkaku-ji is bus-dependent; use the One-Day Pass if traveling from Central Kyoto. |\\n| **11:30** | **Ry\u014dan-ji Temple.** | Zen Garden | 1 hour | Ponder the rock garden. | **CULTURAL DO:** Sit silently at the viewing veranda at Ry\u014dan-ji. It\u2019s not about *finding* meaning, but about *being* present. |\\n| **14:00** | **Daitoku-ji Temple Complex.** | Deeper Zen | 2 hours | Explore sub-temples. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small notepad/sketchbook to capture impressions of the less busy gardens here. |\\n| **17:30** | Walk a segment of the **Philosopher's Path**. | Nature/Reflection | 1 hour | Enjoy late afternoon light. | **BACKUP PLAN (Weather):** If it's raining, substitute the path walk with exploring the covered shopping arcades (Teramachi/Shinkyogoku) near Karasuma Oike. |\\n\\n### Day 4: Western Serenity \u2013 Bamboo & Vegetarian Cuisine\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Arashiyama (West)*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **07:00** | Travel to Arashiyama. | Transit | 45 min | | **LOCAL APP:** Use **Navitime** for highly detailed train/bus routing in Japan, especially when transferring between JR and private lines (like Hankyu for Arashiyama). |\\n| **08:00** | **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | Nature Priority | 1 hour | **Crucial to arrive early.** | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not try to climb into the small adjacent garden areas near the main path for photos\u2014it\u2019s private property. |\\n| **12:30** | **Shojin Ryori Lunch.** | Culinary Tradition | 1.5 hours | Book *shojin ryori* at Shigetsu. | **BUDGET ALTERNATIVE:** If *shojin ryori* is too costly (it can be pricey), find a small shop serving **Tofu/Yudofu** (boiled tofu), a local Arashiyama specialty, which is lighter and cheaper. |\\n| **14:30** | Scenic **Randen Tram** back east. | Experience | 2 hours | | **TRANSPORT TIP:** The Randen Tram is a beautiful, slow ride\u2014a cheaper, more scenic alternative to the Sagano train. |\\n\\n### Day 5: Shogunate Power & Culinary Heart\\n\\n*Geographical Focus: Central Kyoto*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | **Nij\u014d Castle.** | History/Power | 2 hours | Nightingale Floors. | **PACKING TIP:** Bring a small coin purse (\u00a5100 and \u00a5500 coins are essential for many shrine/temple offerings and locker rentals). |\\n| **12:00** | Explore **Nishiki Market**. | Food Culture | 1.5 hours | Graze through samples. | **CULTURAL DON\u2019T:** Do not eat or drink while walking through the market. Find a small bench or designated area to consume your purchase. |\\n| **15:30** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d Temple.** | Art/Worship | 1.5 hours | 1001 statues. | **BACKUP PLAN (Interior Focus):** As this is an indoor activity, it\u2019s perfect for a rainy afternoon. Photography is strictly forbidden inside. |\\n| **19:30** | Dinner in Central Kyoto. | Kyoto Specialty | 2 hours | Try *Kaiseki* or *Saba Zushi*. | **BUDGET TIP (Kaiseki):** If you want the *Kaiseki* experience without the full dinner price, look for *Kaiseki Lunch Sets* on Day 6 instead. |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Dive or Day Trip Exploration\\n\\n*Flex Day: Deep Dive vs. Uji Day Trip*\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Option A (Kyoto):** Nanzen-ji/T\u014d-ji | Zen/Pagoda | Full Day | Lunch near Nanzen-ji. | **BUDGET TIP:** Visit **T\u014d-ji Temple** on the first Sunday of the month if your trip aligns, as it hosts a massive flea market (*Kobo-san* Market) which is free to enter and great for cultural browsing. |\\n| **Option B (Uji Day Trip):** | Tea Focus | Full Day | Matcha Soba/Sweets in Uji. | **TRANSPORT TIP:** Uji is easily accessible on the JR Nara Line (covered by a JR Pass if you have one). If you don't have a pass, ensure you use your IC card. |\\n| **19:30** | Farewell Dinner back in Kyoto. | Celebration | 2 hours | Splurge or revisit. | **CULTURAL DO:** Consider purchasing a small, locally crafted item (pottery, incense) near your favorite temple as a respectful memento. |\\n\\n### Day 7: Reflection and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Focus | Est. Time | Dining Suggestion | **Optimization/Backup Notes** |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **09:00** | Final quiet morning walk. | Reflection | 1.5 hours | | **PACKING TIP:** If you bought liquids/perishables, use the luggage forwarding service (**Takuhaibin**) to send them ahead to the airport or your next hotel for hassle-free departure. |\\n| **10:30** | Last Matcha fix and souvenir gathering. | Last Stops | 1.5 hours | Final coffee/breakfast. | **BUDGET/TIME SAVER:** Buy standardized souvenirs (matcha powder, green tea sweets) at Kyoto Station, which is faster than browsing small shops. |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## IV. Essential Recommendations for Your Cultural Deep Dive\\n\\n### 1. Money-Saving Tips & Budget Alternatives\\n\\n*   **IC Card Strategy:** Purchase an **ICOCA card** immediately upon arrival. While it doesn't offer discounts, it saves significant time compared to buying individual paper tickets for every bus/train transfer.\\n*   **Temple Hopping:** Instead of paying entry fees daily, look for temples that offer special compound passes or explore the *many* free shrines (like Yasaka Shrine).\\n*   **Lunch vs. Dinner:** Japanese fine dining (like *Kaiseki*) is dramatically cheaper at lunchtime. If you want to experience high cuisine, opt for a **Kaiseki Lunch Set** (often 40-50% cheaper than the dinner equivalent).\\n*   **Convenience Store Gems:** For extremely cheap, high-quality breakfast/snacks, utilize 7-Eleven, Lawson, or Family Mart for coffee, sandwiches, and *onigiri*.\\n\\n### 2. Packing Recommendations Specific to Kyoto\\n\\n| Item | Purpose | Cultural/Practical Context |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Comfortable Shoes** | Essential for walking 5\u201310 miles/day on uneven temple paths. | You will be removing shoes frequently; slip-on styles are highly recommended over complicated boots/laces. |\\n| **Layers (Breathable)** | Weather can shift quickly, especially in Spring/Autumn. | Temples are often cool indoors, even if sunny outside. |\\n| **Small Towel/Handkerchief** | Essential for drying hands after using public washrooms or purification fountains. | Public restrooms rarely have paper towels or air dryers. |\\n| **Portable Power Bank** | For constant use of navigation apps (Google Maps, Jorudan). | Kyoto's bus routes are complex; phone battery life is critical. |\\n| **Light Jacket/Rain Shell** | For unpredictable shoulder season weather. | A light, foldable umbrella is easily purchased locally, but a quality waterproof shell is better protection. |\\n\\n### 3. Backup Plans for Weather or Unexpected Situations\\n\\n| Situation | Primary Backup Plan | Cultural/Traditional Alternative |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Heavy Rain/Typhoon** | Focus on Central Kyoto (Nij\u014d Castle, Nishiki Market covered arcades, museums like the Kyoto National Museum). | Attend a traditional craft workshop (pottery, dyeing) held indoors. |\\n| **Extreme Heat/Humidity** | Switch to air-conditioned spaces: Kyoto Station complex (shopping/restaurants), large department stores, or underground shopping malls. | Schedule temple visits for first thing in the morning (7:30 AM) and rest during the 1 PM - 4 PM heat. |\\n| **Major Holiday Crowds (e.g., Golden Week)** | Visit Kyoto\u2019s less-trafficked Zen temples (e.g., Daitokuji sub-temples) or take the Uji day trip (Day 6) early in the morning. | Book all transportation and dinner reservations *at least* 4-6 weeks in advance. |\\n\\n### 4. Local Apps, Websites, or Resources to Download\\n\\n1.  **Google Maps:** Indispensable for real-time bus locations and routes.\\n2.  **Japan Transit Planner (Jorudan) / Navitime:** Best for checking train schedules, platform numbers, and complex transfers.\\n3.  **Google Translate:** Essential, especially for reading small menu items or complex signs at smaller, family-run shops.\\n4.  **Kyoto Bus Navi (Official):** Very helpful for understanding specific bus routes, which cover most tourist sites.\\n\\n### 5. Cultural Do's and Don'ts for Respectful Travel\\n\\n| DO | DON'T |\\n| :--- | :--- |\\n| **DO** Carry a small bag for items removed at temples (e.g., shoes, small purchases). | **DON\u2019T** Take photos of private residences or *Maiko/Geiko* without explicit, clear permission. |\\n| **DO** Say *Sumimasen* (excuse me) before squeezing past someone on a busy train or narrow path. | **DON\u2019T** Eat or drink while walking through the streets or on public transit (unless on the Shinkansen). |\\n| **DO** Use both hands when giving or receiving items (especially money, business cards, or gifts). | **DON\u2019T** Point at people, artifacts, or religious statues with your finger. Use an open hand gesture. |\\n| **DO** Bow slightly when thanking staff or receiving change. | **DON\u2019T** Leave tips\u2014it can be interpreted as confusing or even insulting. |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"intermediate_data\":{\"tool_uses\":[],\"tool_responses\":[],\"intermediate_responses\":[]},\"creation_timestamp\":1763640021.829202,\"rubrics\":null,\"app_details\":null},\"eval_metric_results\":[{\"metric_name\":\"tool_trajectory_avg_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":1.0,\"eval_status\":1,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}},{\"metric_name\":\"response_match_score\",\"threshold\":0.7,\"judge_model_options\":null,\"criterion\":null,\"score\":0.5721518987341772,\"eval_status\":2,\"details\":{\"rubric_scores\":null}}]}],\"session_id\":\"___eval___session___8487ebc8-0656-4496-9533-2d991ea5b8f4\",\"session_details\":{\"id\":\"___eval___session___8487ebc8-0656-4496-9533-2d991ea5b8f4\",\"app_name\":\"TravelPlannerAgent\",\"user_id\":\"user\",\"state\":{\"travel_itinerary\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent has processed the destination research and formulated a comprehensive 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto.\\n\\nThis itinerary balances iconic sightseeing with immersive cultural activities, ensuring efficient travel by grouping attractions geographically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Immersion\\n\\n**Focus:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Recommended Travel Season:** Spring (April/May) or Autumn (October/November)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor the best balance of accessibility, atmosphere, and proximity to dining:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama/Gion Area (Recommended for Culture):** Closest to many iconic temples, traditional streets, and evening atmosphere. Excellent for soaking in the old-Kyoto vibe.\\n2.  **Kawaramachi/Downtown Area:** Central hub, best for transit links (buses/subway) and modern dining variety.\\n3.  **Central Kyoto (Near Kyoto Station):** Best for day trips and ease of arrival/departure, but slightly less charming for evening ambiance.\\n\\n## Estimated Daily Budget Breakdown (Excluding Accommodation)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range estimate for one person, assuming IC card usage for transit and a mix of casual and slightly nicer dinners.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Daily Cost (USD, Approx.) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Entry Fees** | \u00a52,500 - \u00a54,000 | $17 - $27 | Based on 3-4 temple entries per day. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 - \u00a510,000 | $40 - $67 | Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Snacks. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | $7 - $10 | Daily bus/subway use (using IC Card). |\\n| **Cultural Activity** | Varies (Budgeted separately below) | - | Tea Ceremony, special workshops. |\\n| **TOTAL (Excl. Activity/Hotels)** | **\u00a59,500 - \u00a515,500** | **$64 - $104** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed 7-Day Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and The Spiritual Heart of Eastern Kyoto (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive in Kyoto, check into accommodation, purchase IC Card (e.g., ICOCA). | 3 hours | Quick breakfast near the station or hotel. | Familiarize yourself with the local bus network immediately. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** Explore the main hall and wooden veranda. | 2 hours | Lunch near Kiyomizu-dera (Try **Yuba**\u2014tofu skin specialties). | Be prepared for crowds; observe purification rituals at the Otowa Waterfall. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk the historic **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets. | 1.5 hours | Stop for Matcha soft serve or Japanese sweets. | Excellent for photography; watch for tourists photographing Maiko/Geiko respectfully. |\\n| **Evening** | Explore **Gion District** and **Hanamikoji Street**. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Traditional *Kaiseki* (if budget allows) or affordable *Okonomiyaki* in the Pontocho Alley area. | Keep noise levels low in Gion; do not approach or stop Geiko/Maiko for photos. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kiyomizu-dera Entry: \u00a5400 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Golden Beauty and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel North. **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. | 1.5 hours | Grab a quick *Onigiri* (rice ball) for breakfast on the go. | Arrive right at opening (9:00 AM) to experience the reflection on the pond with fewer crowds. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | Lunch near the temple area (simple noodle shop recommended). | Sit quietly and observe the rock garden; avoid touching the stones. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former Shogun\u2019s residence, focusing on the Nightingale Floors. | 2 hours | Tea/Coffee break. | Listen for the squeaking \\\"nightingale floors\\\" designed as security alarms. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore Nishiki Market area for diverse street food or casual *Izakaya* dining. | Nishiki Market usually closes early, so focus on nearby restaurants. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kinkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500, Ryoan-ji Entry: \u00a5600, Nijo Castle Entry: \u00a51,040 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Immersion in Tradition \u2013 Tea Ceremony and Craft\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Cultural Workshop:** Participate in a formal **Tea Ceremony (Chanoyu)**. | 2 hours | Light breakfast before the ceremony. | **Crucial Cultural Deep Dive:** Book in advance. Focus on the precise movements and philosophy. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Explore the dry sand garden (\\\"Sea of Silver Sand\\\"). | 1.5 hours | Lunch near Ginkaku-ji (Look for shops selling traditional Kyoto sweets). | A much calmer contrast to Kinkaku-ji; enjoy the moss garden paths. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A reflective walk along the canal. | 1.5 hours | Stop at local craft shops along the path for browsing. | Especially beautiful during Sakura or autumn foliage season. |\\n| **Evening** | Free time/Relaxation. | Flexible | **Dinner:** Try Kyoto-style *Obanzai* (home-style local cooking). | Use this evening to relax after two intense days of sightseeing. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Ginkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000 - \u00a56,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Thousand Gates and Southern Majesty (Fushimi & Nara Option)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Hike through the thousands of red *torii* gates. | 3 - 4 hours | Early light breakfast. Purchase local street snacks (like *Inari Sushi*) near the base. | Go as early as possible (before 8 AM) to experience the gates without massive crowds. You do not need to hike to the very top. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near Fushimi Inari Station. | 1 hour | Lunch at a local spot specializing in eel or simple udon. | |\\n| **Afternoon (Option A)**| **Day Trip to Nara:** Visit Todai-ji Temple (Giant Buddha) and interact with the friendly wild deer. | 5 hours (Including travel) | Street snacks in Nara (e.g., *mochi* pounding demonstration). | Nara is 45 mins by JR train from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon (Option B)**| **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d:** Visit the hall containing 1,001 statues of Kannon. | 1.5 hours | Relaxing afternoon back in Kyoto. | Photography is strictly forbidden inside the hall; marvel at the precision of the statues. |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner:** Explore dining options around Kyoto Station (great for variety and efficiency). | Flexible | Dinner: Ramen street in Kyoto Station or Isetan department store dining floors. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Fushimi Inari: Free. Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d: \u00a5600. Nara (Todai-ji): \u00a5600. | | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Bamboo Groves and Western Serenity (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama (JR Saga Line recommended). **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | 2 hours | Breakfast at the hotel or a bakery near Saga-Arashiyama station. | Visit the Bamboo Grove immediately upon arrival (before 9 AM) for the best experience. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple:** Explore the main temple and its extensive landscape garden. | 2 hours | Lunch near the Katsura River. Try vegetarian shojin ryori (monk's cuisine) if available. | A UNESCO site; the garden seamlessly integrates the surrounding mountains. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Cross the **Togetsukyo Bridge** and explore the smaller, quieter temples on the western bank (e.g., Okochi Sanso Villa). | 2.5 hours | Stop for sweets or green tea near the river. | Okochi Sanso (former actor's villa) offers spectacular views and includes tea in the entry fee. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore the dining area near your hotel or try a specialized *Tempura* restaurant. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Tenry\u016b-ji Entry: \u00a5500 (Garden only), Okochi Sanso: \u00a51,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Culture & Artistic Exploration\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Kyoto National Museum** or **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (depending on interest). | 3 hours | Breakfast/Brunch near the museum area. | Great for a slightly less temple-focused cultural day. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch. | 1 hour | Ramen or Curry. | |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Area Revisit (Optional):** Explore smaller, less-visited sub-temples (like K\u014ddai-ji) or traditional pottery shops in Kiyomizu-zaka. | 3 hours | Second chance for a traditional sweet treat. | This allows time for souvenir shopping focusing on local crafts (pottery, fans). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| **Shinto Tradition Immersion:** Visit a smaller, local shrine (e.g., Kitano Tenman-g\u016b if time permits) to observe daily rituals. | 1.5 hours | | Observe the *ema* (prayer plaques) and local customs. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner:** Splurge on a memorable Kyoto dining experience (e.g., high-quality Wagyu or specialized Sushi). | Flexible | **Dinner:** Reservation highly recommended for the farewell meal. | Reflect on the trip; purchase any last-minute gifts. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Museum Entry: Approx. \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Reflections and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Morning Meditation/Temple Visit:** Revisit a favorite quiet spot (like a smaller Zen garden) or visit a local market for breakfast. | 2 hours | A final, classic Japanese breakfast set (grilled fish, rice, miso soup). | Use this time for quiet reflection before the busy travel day. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Final packing and check-out. | 1.5 hours | | Ensure all transportation tickets for onward travel are ready. |\\n| **Noon/Afternoon**| Travel to Kansai International Airport (KIX) or onward destination. | Varies | Quick and easy lunch near Kyoto Station before departure. | The JR Haruka Express is the most efficient way to KIX from Kyoto Station (approx. 75 minutes). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Major Budget Estimates (7 Days, Per Person)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (USD) | Mid-Range Estimate (USD) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (7 Nights)** | $700 (Budget Hostel/Guesthouse) | $1,200 (Mid-range Hotel/Airbnb) | Highly variable based on season. |\\n| **Food & Dining (7 Days)** | $300 ($43/day) | $550 ($78/day) | Includes one minor splurge meal. |\\n| **Activities/Entry Fees** | $100 | $150 | Covers all temple/museum entries researched above. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences (Tea Ceremony)** | $40 | $60 | |\\n| **Local Transport (IC Card Top-ups)** | $50 | $70 | Covers local travel within Kyoto city limits. |\\n| **Intercity Travel (e.g., to KIX)** | $30 | $40 | One-way estimate to major airport/station. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE (Excluding Flights)** | **$1,220** | **$2,070** | |\",\"__llm_request_key__\":\"a73ab89f-860d-44e5-816d-3c9b1bafdd22\",\"destination_research\":\"Here is a comprehensive guide for planning your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary Planning\\n\\nKyoto, the former imperial capital of Japan, is the quintessential destination for immersing oneself in traditional Japanese culture. This plan balances iconic sites with deeper, more reflective experiences.\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are during **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Famous for **cherry blossoms** (late March to early April). Weather is generally warm and comfortable, though there can be some rain, especially in late May.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Known for stunning **fall foliage** (peaking in November). The weather is cool and comfortable, with generally sunny skies.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season usually running from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, offering a more tranquil atmosphere.\\n\\n**Recommendation for your trip:** Aim for **October/November** or **April/May** for the best combination of weather and scenery to enjoy outdoor temples and nature walks. Be aware that these are peak seasons, meaning larger crowds and potentially higher costs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions and Must-See Locations\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, focus on UNESCO World Heritage Sites and areas rich in tradition.\\n\\n| Category | Must-See Location(s) | Cultural Significance/Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple, its top two floors covered in gold leaf, reflected in a pond. |\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its impressive 13-meter-high wooden veranda, built without a single nail, offering views over Kyoto. |\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its exquisite Zen garden, including the Sea of Silver Sand. |\\n| **Nature & Zen** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple (Arashiyama)** | A leading Zen temple boasting one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. |\\n| | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Home to the world-famous rock garden, a prime example of Zen philosophy in design. |\\n| **Unique Spiritual Sites**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | Famous for its thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates that wind up the mountain trail. |\\n| **Historical Districts** | **Gion District** | Kyoto's famed geisha district, best for experiencing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. |\\n| **Other Key Sites** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** | Houses 1,001 statues of Kannon in its long wooden hall. |\\n| | **Nijo Castle** | A former residence of the Tokugawa Shogunate, featuring intricate paintings and \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values its rich history and traditions, making cultural respect paramount.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Respect Sacred Spaces:** Keep your voice low; temples are places for worship and contemplation.\\n    *   **Purification:** Use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) before entering: cleanse your left hand, then right, rinse your mouth (not touching the ladle to your lips), and finally, rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering certain temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* mats.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography is often prohibited inside buildings or in certain areas. Do not use a flash or take photos of worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Offerings:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n*   **General Conduct:**\\n    *   **Politeness:** Use polite language like *\\\"Arigat\u014d gozaimasu\\\"* (Thank you very much) and *\\\"Sumimasen\\\"* (Pardon me/Sorry).\\n    *   **Noise:** Keep your voice down in public spaces, on public transport, and especially at temples.\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is **not** customary in Japan; a simple \\\"thanks\\\" suffices.\\n    *   **Trash:** Kyoto is very clean; carry a small bag for your trash as public bins are rare. Do not litter.\\n    *   **Geisha/Maiko:** Do not stop, follow, or act like paparazzi to photograph Geiko (Geisha) or Maiko; they are often on their way to appointments.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony:** This is a must-do experience to dive deep into tradition. Book a ceremony in advance, often available near major temples or in the Gion/Higashiyama area. Be mindful of the quiet, precise movements required during the ceremony.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options Within Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto has a good public transport network, though unlike Tokyo, buses often play a larger role in reaching specific landmarks.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Often the **best way** to reach temples and attractions not near train/subway stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji). Be aware of paying etiquette: generally enter at the back, exit at the front, and pay a fixed fare upon exiting (though drivers may not give change, so have coins ready).\\n*   **Subway and Trains:** Excellent for longer distances and North-South/East-West travel. Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W). Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu) connect to key spots like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) for easy payment across nearly all public transport.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for walking, especially in historic districts like Higashiyama and Arashiyama. Biking is also a popular option.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Lost items are frequently returned.\\n*   **Emergency Contacts:**\\n    *   **Police:** Dial **110**.\\n    *   **Medical Emergency/Fire:** Dial **119**.\\n*   **Health/Environment:**\\n    *   Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   Be aware of potential **bear sightings** if hiking near mountainous areas (like Kurama/Kibune). Travel in groups and carry noise-making items if venturing far into the mountains.\\n*   **Documentation:**\\n    *   **Visas:** Check the latest requirements with the nearest Japanese embassy, as visa exemption rules can change.\\n    *   **Insurance:** It is highly advisable to take out **travel insurance** covering medical treatment and emergencies.\\n    *   **Copies:** Keep copies of important documents (passport, insurance) separate from the originals.\\n*   **Local Laws/Customs:** Always follow local laws, religious restrictions, and customs, particularly regarding behavior at sacred sites.\\n\\n---\\nThis framework should provide a solid foundation for designing your 7-day cultural immersion in Kyoto. You can allocate days to specific districts (e.g., Eastern Kyoto for Higashiyama/Kiyomizu-dera, Western Kyoto for Arashiyama/Tenryu-ji) to maximize travel efficiency.\"},\"events\":[{\"model_version\":null,\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Plan a 7-day cultural deep-dive travel experience in Kyoto focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"user\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":null,\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":null,\"usage_metadata\":null,\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-609c22d2-986f-4d55-b284-f54138831c29\",\"author\":\"user\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"cbddcdd9-a0af-45ef-bf20-d4bd56607098\",\"timestamp\":1763640864.90837},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"Here is a comprehensive guide for planning your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary Planning\\n\\nKyoto, the former imperial capital of Japan, is the quintessential destination for immersing oneself in traditional Japanese culture. This plan balances iconic sites with deeper, more reflective experiences.\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are during **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Famous for **cherry blossoms** (late March to early April). Weather is generally warm and comfortable, though there can be some rain, especially in late May.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Known for stunning **fall foliage** (peaking in November). The weather is cool and comfortable, with generally sunny skies.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season usually running from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, offering a more tranquil atmosphere.\\n\\n**Recommendation for your trip:** Aim for **October/November** or **April/May** for the best combination of weather and scenery to enjoy outdoor temples and nature walks. Be aware that these are peak seasons, meaning larger crowds and potentially higher costs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions and Must-See Locations\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, focus on UNESCO World Heritage Sites and areas rich in tradition.\\n\\n| Category | Must-See Location(s) | Cultural Significance/Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple, its top two floors covered in gold leaf, reflected in a pond. |\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its impressive 13-meter-high wooden veranda, built without a single nail, offering views over Kyoto. |\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its exquisite Zen garden, including the Sea of Silver Sand. |\\n| **Nature & Zen** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple (Arashiyama)** | A leading Zen temple boasting one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. |\\n| | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Home to the world-famous rock garden, a prime example of Zen philosophy in design. |\\n| **Unique Spiritual Sites**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | Famous for its thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates that wind up the mountain trail. |\\n| **Historical Districts** | **Gion District** | Kyoto's famed geisha district, best for experiencing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. |\\n| **Other Key Sites** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** | Houses 1,001 statues of Kannon in its long wooden hall. |\\n| | **Nijo Castle** | A former residence of the Tokugawa Shogunate, featuring intricate paintings and \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values its rich history and traditions, making cultural respect paramount.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Respect Sacred Spaces:** Keep your voice low; temples are places for worship and contemplation.\\n    *   **Purification:** Use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) before entering: cleanse your left hand, then right, rinse your mouth (not touching the ladle to your lips), and finally, rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering certain temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* mats.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography is often prohibited inside buildings or in certain areas. Do not use a flash or take photos of worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Offerings:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n*   **General Conduct:**\\n    *   **Politeness:** Use polite language like *\\\"Arigat\u014d gozaimasu\\\"* (Thank you very much) and *\\\"Sumimasen\\\"* (Pardon me/Sorry).\\n    *   **Noise:** Keep your voice down in public spaces, on public transport, and especially at temples.\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is **not** customary in Japan; a simple \\\"thanks\\\" suffices.\\n    *   **Trash:** Kyoto is very clean; carry a small bag for your trash as public bins are rare. Do not litter.\\n    *   **Geisha/Maiko:** Do not stop, follow, or act like paparazzi to photograph Geiko (Geisha) or Maiko; they are often on their way to appointments.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony:** This is a must-do experience to dive deep into tradition. Book a ceremony in advance, often available near major temples or in the Gion/Higashiyama area. Be mindful of the quiet, precise movements required during the ceremony.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options Within Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto has a good public transport network, though unlike Tokyo, buses often play a larger role in reaching specific landmarks.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Often the **best way** to reach temples and attractions not near train/subway stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji). Be aware of paying etiquette: generally enter at the back, exit at the front, and pay a fixed fare upon exiting (though drivers may not give change, so have coins ready).\\n*   **Subway and Trains:** Excellent for longer distances and North-South/East-West travel. Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W). Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu) connect to key spots like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) for easy payment across nearly all public transport.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for walking, especially in historic districts like Higashiyama and Arashiyama. Biking is also a popular option.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Lost items are frequently returned.\\n*   **Emergency Contacts:**\\n    *   **Police:** Dial **110**.\\n    *   **Medical Emergency/Fire:** Dial **119**.\\n*   **Health/Environment:**\\n    *   Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   Be aware of potential **bear sightings** if hiking near mountainous areas (like Kurama/Kibune). Travel in groups and carry noise-making items if venturing far into the mountains.\\n*   **Documentation:**\\n    *   **Visas:** Check the latest requirements with the nearest Japanese embassy, as visa exemption rules can change.\\n    *   **Insurance:** It is highly advisable to take out **travel insurance** covering medical treatment and emergencies.\\n    *   **Copies:** Keep copies of important documents (passport, insurance) separate from the originals.\\n*   **Local Laws/Customs:** Always follow local laws, religious restrictions, and customs, particularly regarding behavior at sacred sites.\\n\\n---\\nThis framework should provide a solid foundation for designing your 7-day cultural immersion in Kyoto. You can allocate days to specific districts (e.g., Eastern Kyoto for Higashiyama/Kiyomizu-dera, Western Kyoto for Arashiyama/Tenryu-ji) to maximize travel efficiency.\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":{\"google_maps_widget_context_token\":null,\"grounding_chunks\":[{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"insidekyoto.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG-uWySDvcZnaGihJ0ky4Imc7o7jyRmOGlYciSyV5tfslwbmReRPxtjXHob96n2LdkT3VdXG7M2af6kFCQE39_Vp04UL5W-q9SKjITgzWd3o5eAL9iPaTD7u1PNZ38g-eI_foQG19Iy_LqVBCu6u04GPHgb49nuBvow\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"japanhighlights.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHlzaHLVDUjxs3tvUcyMESxc8e23SHcshydvpAIqqyIvlH3wXsDPuksIICwQBio8MPB6_mAgSy25KZ-Vro1KizyCTmyuz4w__JpMa421InZmlo5nUCFrDINXxsy5TUEgfyFwORprETVqHDQpAx7box4ICcPBNW2f7fP\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"santorinidave.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE1-uwa1CqGr2qQZwLRnD126XldsMIlR3DmdpxugBx6kFKEmp8Qd5hayPPi1SQqTuHnlZkb62OczpfvyI9CywHKz3l7x8S_ZjJe8cn1VuJp5xk0f1RQaHfGBakx1dSybS7CKRX5nUz8uTkyyaqk\"}},{\"maps\":null,\"retrieved_context\":null,\"web\":{\"domain\":null,\"title\":\"trip.com\",\"uri\":\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE-yi0coHyYwmGGao3Kh7VT27ZawpSW0QmX44g