G0QaQGU7l1jjN-5VFJYuiYApaCNwL2xh1Jf2nVjBZVIBN3r03VshIQHfpN6oexfLVwXjs90DjHo9cZkyOvvP9ajd-fVe3aoiUgr9dz2_Co_jF4ro5QkHOoEyZpdE7NKJeR7CGJ-j0A==\\\">top temples and cultural sites in Kyoto</a>\\n    <a class=\\\"chip\\\" href=\\\"https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGbIJiWrGCl9DV5WlZoIjQH3npSvgw9ZWpopLHe-wriMXqAgC5vrcruylESFga4TNOMcppCCGWhz-H91c71SITQWl535ZHzUw-OYo3u3PPHn4vWDzI_YSCY8DIHWzhi8ZhCdRomQJizls_44Ko4-IefmcpeHlimjLSJuCJPrRKXuVQiJqB-uauz5kQpuCKs2bEdEjecu2Ms7kGfTHPxeUaeqoO-L0G75g==\\\">Kyoto safety and travel requirements</a>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\",\"sdk_blob\":null},\"source_flagging_uris\":null,\"web_search_queries\":[\"best time to visit Kyoto weather\",\"top temples and cultural sites in Kyoto\",\"Kyoto local culture customs etiquette\",\"transportation options in Kyoto\",\"Kyoto safety and travel requirements\"]},\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"a305092c-d982-4254-9969-bdf693b65b2d\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":1749,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":142,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"thoughts_token_count\":null,\"tool_use_prompt_token_count\":142,\"tool_use_prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":142}],\"total_token_count\":2033,\"traffic_type\":null},\"live_session_resumption_update\":null,\"input_transcription\":null,\"output_transcription\":null,\"avg_logprobs\":null,\"logprobs_result\":null,\"cache_metadata\":null,\"citation_metadata\":null,\"invocation_id\":\"e-609c22d2-986f-4d55-b284-f54138831c29\",\"author\":\"DestinationResearchAgent\",\"actions\":{\"skip_summarization\":null,\"state_delta\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"a305092c-d982-4254-9969-bdf693b65b2d\",\"destination_research\":\"Here is a comprehensive guide for planning your 7-day cultural deep-dive in Kyoto, focusing on temples, traditions, tea ceremonies, and nature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive in Kyoto Itinerary Planning\\n\\nKyoto, the former imperial capital of Japan, is the quintessential destination for immersing oneself in traditional Japanese culture. This plan balances iconic sites with deeper, more reflective experiences.\\n\\n### 1. Best Time to Visit and Weather Patterns\\n\\nThe best times to visit Kyoto are during **Spring (March to May)** and **Autumn (October to November)**, as these seasons offer the most pleasant weather for sightseeing and beautiful natural scenery.\\n\\n*   **Spring (March - May):** Famous for **cherry blossoms** (late March to early April). Weather is generally warm and comfortable, though there can be some rain, especially in late May.\\n*   **Autumn (October - November):** Known for stunning **fall foliage** (peaking in November). The weather is cool and comfortable, with generally sunny skies.\\n*   **Summer (June - August):** Hot and humid, with a rainy season usually running from mid-June to late July.\\n*   **Winter (December - February):** Cold, offering a more tranquil atmosphere.\\n\\n**Recommendation for your trip:** Aim for **October/November** or **April/May** for the best combination of weather and scenery to enjoy outdoor temples and nature walks. Be aware that these are peak seasons, meaning larger crowds and potentially higher costs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 2. Top Attractions and Must-See Locations\\n\\nFor a cultural deep-dive, focus on UNESCO World Heritage Sites and areas rich in tradition.\\n\\n| Category | Must-See Location(s) | Cultural Significance/Highlight |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Iconic Temples** | **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)** | A stunning Zen temple, its top two floors covered in gold leaf, reflected in a pond. |\\n| | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple** | Famous for its impressive 13-meter-high wooden veranda, built without a single nail, offering views over Kyoto. |\\n| | **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)** | Known for its exquisite Zen garden, including the Sea of Silver Sand. |\\n| **Nature & Zen** | **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple (Arashiyama)** | A leading Zen temple boasting one of the city's oldest and most celebrated landscape gardens. |\\n| | **Ryoan-ji Temple** | Home to the world-famous rock garden, a prime example of Zen philosophy in design. |\\n| **Unique Spiritual Sites**| **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine** | Famous for its thousands of vibrant red *torii* gates that wind up the mountain trail. |\\n| **Historical Districts** | **Gion District** | Kyoto's famed geisha district, best for experiencing traditional wooden *machiya* houses. |\\n| **Other Key Sites** | **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d** | Houses 1,001 statues of Kannon in its long wooden hall. |\\n| | **Nijo Castle** | A former residence of the Tokugawa Shogunate, featuring intricate paintings and \\\"nightingale floors.\\\" |\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 3. Local Culture, Customs, and Etiquette Tips\\n\\nKyoto highly values its rich history and traditions, making cultural respect paramount.\\n\\n*   **Temples and Shrines Etiquette:**\\n    *   **Respect Sacred Spaces:** Keep your voice low; temples are places for worship and contemplation.\\n    *   **Purification:** Use the *temizuya* (purification fountain) before entering: cleanse your left hand, then right, rinse your mouth (not touching the ladle to your lips), and finally, rinse the ladle handle.\\n    *   **Shoes:** Be prepared to remove your shoes before entering certain temple buildings, especially those with *tatami* mats.\\n    *   **Photography:** Observe signs carefully; photography is often prohibited inside buildings or in certain areas. Do not use a flash or take photos of worshippers without permission.\\n    *   **Offerings:** Bow slightly when entering temple grounds as a sign of respect.\\n*   **General Conduct:**\\n    *   **Politeness:** Use polite language like *\\\"Arigat\u014d gozaimasu\\\"* (Thank you very much) and *\\\"Sumimasen\\\"* (Pardon me/Sorry).\\n    *   **Noise:** Keep your voice down in public spaces, on public transport, and especially at temples.\\n    *   **Tipping:** Tipping is **not** customary in Japan; a simple \\\"thanks\\\" suffices.\\n    *   **Trash:** Kyoto is very clean; carry a small bag for your trash as public bins are rare. Do not litter.\\n    *   **Geisha/Maiko:** Do not stop, follow, or act like paparazzi to photograph Geiko (Geisha) or Maiko; they are often on their way to appointments.\\n*   **Tea Ceremony:** This is a must-do experience to dive deep into tradition. Book a ceremony in advance, often available near major temples or in the Gion/Higashiyama area. Be mindful of the quiet, precise movements required during the ceremony.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 4. Transportation Options Within Kyoto\\n\\nKyoto has a good public transport network, though unlike Tokyo, buses often play a larger role in reaching specific landmarks.\\n\\n*   **Buses:** Often the **best way** to reach temples and attractions not near train/subway stations (e.g., Kinkaku-ji, Ryoan-ji). Be aware of paying etiquette: generally enter at the back, exit at the front, and pay a fixed fare upon exiting (though drivers may not give change, so have coins ready).\\n*   **Subway and Trains:** Excellent for longer distances and North-South/East-West travel. Kyoto has two subway lines (Karasuma N-S, Tozai E-W). Local train lines (JR, Keihan, Hankyu) connect to key spots like Arashiyama and Fushimi Inari.\\n*   **IC Cards:** Purchase an **IC Card** (like ICOCA, Suica, or PASMO) for easy payment across nearly all public transport.\\n*   **Walking/Biking:** Kyoto is relatively flat and great for walking, especially in historic districts like Higashiyama and Arashiyama. Biking is also a popular option.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### 5. Safety Considerations and Travel Requirements\\n\\nKyoto is generally considered an **extremely safe city** with low crime rates.\\n\\n*   **General Safety:** It is safe to walk the streets at night, even as a solo traveler. Lost items are frequently returned.\\n*   **Emergency Contacts:**\\n    *   **Police:** Dial **110**.\\n    *   **Medical Emergency/Fire:** Dial **119**.\\n*   **Health/Environment:**\\n    *   Tap water is safe to drink.\\n    *   Be aware of potential **bear sightings** if hiking near mountainous areas (like Kurama/Kibune). Travel in groups and carry noise-making items if venturing far into the mountains.\\n*   **Documentation:**\\n    *   **Visas:** Check the latest requirements with the nearest Japanese embassy, as visa exemption rules can change.\\n    *   **Insurance:** It is highly advisable to take out **travel insurance** covering medical treatment and emergencies.\\n    *   **Copies:** Keep copies of important documents (passport, insurance) separate from the originals.\\n*   **Local Laws/Customs:** Always follow local laws, religious restrictions, and customs, particularly regarding behavior at sacred sites.\\n\\n---\\nThis framework should provide a solid foundation for designing your 7-day cultural immersion in Kyoto. You can allocate days to specific districts (e.g., Eastern Kyoto for Higashiyama/Kiyomizu-dera, Western Kyoto for Arashiyama/Tenryu-ji) to maximize travel efficiency.\"},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"4ae9f38b-0bf9-4d81-b527-f849535ed74a\",\"timestamp\":1763640864.908915},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ItineraryBuilderAgent has processed the destination research and formulated a comprehensive 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto.\\n\\nThis itinerary balances iconic sightseeing with immersive cultural activities, ensuring efficient travel by grouping attractions geographically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Immersion\\n\\n**Focus:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Recommended Travel Season:** Spring (April/May) or Autumn (October/November)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor the best balance of accessibility, atmosphere, and proximity to dining:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama/Gion Area (Recommended for Culture):** Closest to many iconic temples, traditional streets, and evening atmosphere. Excellent for soaking in the old-Kyoto vibe.\\n2.  **Kawaramachi/Downtown Area:** Central hub, best for transit links (buses/subway) and modern dining variety.\\n3.  **Central Kyoto (Near Kyoto Station):** Best for day trips and ease of arrival/departure, but slightly less charming for evening ambiance.\\n\\n## Estimated Daily Budget Breakdown (Excluding Accommodation)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range estimate for one person, assuming IC card usage for transit and a mix of casual and slightly nicer dinners.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated Daily Cost (JPY) | Estimated Daily Cost (USD, Approx.) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Entry Fees** | \u00a52,500 - \u00a54,000 | $17 - $27 | Based on 3-4 temple entries per day. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 - \u00a510,000 | $40 - $67 | Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Snacks. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | $7 - $10 | Daily bus/subway use (using IC Card). |\\n| **Cultural Activity** | Varies (Budgeted separately below) | - | Tea Ceremony, special workshops. |\\n| **TOTAL (Excl. Activity/Hotels)** | **\u00a59,500 - \u00a515,500** | **$64 - $104** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed 7-Day Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and The Spiritual Heart of Eastern Kyoto (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive in Kyoto, check into accommodation, purchase IC Card (e.g., ICOCA). | 3 hours | Quick breakfast near the station or hotel. | Familiarize yourself with the local bus network immediately. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** Explore the main hall and wooden veranda. | 2 hours | Lunch near Kiyomizu-dera (Try **Yuba**\u2014tofu skin specialties). | Be prepared for crowds; observe purification rituals at the Otowa Waterfall. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk the historic **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets. | 1.5 hours | Stop for Matcha soft serve or Japanese sweets. | Excellent for photography; watch for tourists photographing Maiko/Geiko respectfully. |\\n| **Evening** | Explore **Gion District** and **Hanamikoji Street**. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Traditional *Kaiseki* (if budget allows) or affordable *Okonomiyaki* in the Pontocho Alley area. | Keep noise levels low in Gion; do not approach or stop Geiko/Maiko for photos. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kiyomizu-dera Entry: \u00a5400 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Golden Beauty and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel North. **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. | 1.5 hours | Grab a quick *Onigiri* (rice ball) for breakfast on the go. | Arrive right at opening (9:00 AM) to experience the reflection on the pond with fewer crowds. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | Lunch near the temple area (simple noodle shop recommended). | Sit quietly and observe the rock garden; avoid touching the stones. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former Shogun\u2019s residence, focusing on the Nightingale Floors. | 2 hours | Tea/Coffee break. | Listen for the squeaking \\\"nightingale floors\\\" designed as security alarms. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore Nishiki Market area for diverse street food or casual *Izakaya* dining. | Nishiki Market usually closes early, so focus on nearby restaurants. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kinkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500, Ryoan-ji Entry: \u00a5600, Nijo Castle Entry: \u00a51,040 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Immersion in Tradition \u2013 Tea Ceremony and Craft\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Cultural Workshop:** Participate in a formal **Tea Ceremony (Chanoyu)**. | 2 hours | Light breakfast before the ceremony. | **Crucial Cultural Deep Dive:** Book in advance. Focus on the precise movements and philosophy. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Explore the dry sand garden (\\\"Sea of Silver Sand\\\"). | 1.5 hours | Lunch near Ginkaku-ji (Look for shops selling traditional Kyoto sweets). | A much calmer contrast to Kinkaku-ji; enjoy the moss garden paths. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A reflective walk along the canal. | 1.5 hours | Stop at local craft shops along the path for browsing. | Especially beautiful during Sakura or autumn foliage season. |\\n| **Evening** | Free time/Relaxation. | Flexible | **Dinner:** Try Kyoto-style *Obanzai* (home-style local cooking). | Use this evening to relax after two intense days of sightseeing. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Ginkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000 - \u00a56,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Thousand Gates and Southern Majesty (Fushimi & Nara Option)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Hike through the thousands of red *torii* gates. | 3 - 4 hours | Early light breakfast. Purchase local street snacks (like *Inari Sushi*) near the base. | Go as early as possible (before 8 AM) to experience the gates without massive crowds. You do not need to hike to the very top. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near Fushimi Inari Station. | 1 hour | Lunch at a local spot specializing in eel or simple udon. | |\\n| **Afternoon (Option A)**| **Day Trip to Nara:** Visit Todai-ji Temple (Giant Buddha) and interact with the friendly wild deer. | 5 hours (Including travel) | Street snacks in Nara (e.g., *mochi* pounding demonstration). | Nara is 45 mins by JR train from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon (Option B)**| **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d:** Visit the hall containing 1,001 statues of Kannon. | 1.5 hours | Relaxing afternoon back in Kyoto. | Photography is strictly forbidden inside the hall; marvel at the precision of the statues. |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner:** Explore dining options around Kyoto Station (great for variety and efficiency). | Flexible | Dinner: Ramen street in Kyoto Station or Isetan department store dining floors. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Fushimi Inari: Free. Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d: \u00a5600. Nara (Todai-ji): \u00a5600. | | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Bamboo Groves and Western Serenity (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama (JR Saga Line recommended). **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | 2 hours | Breakfast at the hotel or a bakery near Saga-Arashiyama station. | Visit the Bamboo Grove immediately upon arrival (before 9 AM) for the best experience. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple:** Explore the main temple and its extensive landscape garden. | 2 hours | Lunch near the Katsura River. Try vegetarian shojin ryori (monk's cuisine) if available. | A UNESCO site; the garden seamlessly integrates the surrounding mountains. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Cross the **Togetsukyo Bridge** and explore the smaller, quieter temples on the western bank (e.g., Okochi Sanso Villa). | 2.5 hours | Stop for sweets or green tea near the river. | Okochi Sanso (former actor's villa) offers spectacular views and includes tea in the entry fee. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore the dining area near your hotel or try a specialized *Tempura* restaurant. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Tenry\u016b-ji Entry: \u00a5500 (Garden only), Okochi Sanso: \u00a51,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Culture & Artistic Exploration\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Kyoto National Museum** or **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (depending on interest). | 3 hours | Breakfast/Brunch near the museum area. | Great for a slightly less temple-focused cultural day. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch. | 1 hour | Ramen or Curry. | |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Area Revisit (Optional):** Explore smaller, less-visited sub-temples (like K\u014ddai-ji) or traditional pottery shops in Kiyomizu-zaka. | 3 hours | Second chance for a traditional sweet treat. | This allows time for souvenir shopping focusing on local crafts (pottery, fans). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| **Shinto Tradition Immersion:** Visit a smaller, local shrine (e.g., Kitano Tenman-g\u016b if time permits) to observe daily rituals. | 1.5 hours | | Observe the *ema* (prayer plaques) and local customs. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner:** Splurge on a memorable Kyoto dining experience (e.g., high-quality Wagyu or specialized Sushi). | Flexible | **Dinner:** Reservation highly recommended for the farewell meal. | Reflect on the trip; purchase any last-minute gifts. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Museum Entry: Approx. \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Reflections and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Morning Meditation/Temple Visit:** Revisit a favorite quiet spot (like a smaller Zen garden) or visit a local market for breakfast. | 2 hours | A final, classic Japanese breakfast set (grilled fish, rice, miso soup). | Use this time for quiet reflection before the busy travel day. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Final packing and check-out. | 1.5 hours | | Ensure all transportation tickets for onward travel are ready. |\\n| **Noon/Afternoon**| Travel to Kansai International Airport (KIX) or onward destination. | Varies | Quick and easy lunch near Kyoto Station before departure. | The JR Haruka Express is the most efficient way to KIX from Kyoto Station (approx. 75 minutes). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Major Budget Estimates (7 Days, Per Person)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (USD) | Mid-Range Estimate (USD) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (7 Nights)** | $700 (Budget Hostel/Guesthouse) | $1,200 (Mid-range Hotel/Airbnb) | Highly variable based on season. |\\n| **Food & Dining (7 Days)** | $300 ($43/day) | $550 ($78/day) | Includes one minor splurge meal. |\\n| **Activities/Entry Fees** | $100 | $150 | Covers all temple/museum entries researched above. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences (Tea Ceremony)** | $40 | $60 | |\\n| **Local Transport (IC Card Top-ups)** | $50 | $70 | Covers local travel within Kyoto city limits. |\\n| **Intercity Travel (e.g., to KIX)** | $30 | $40 | One-way estimate to major airport/station. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE (Excluding Flights)** | **$1,220** | **$2,070** | |\",\"thought\":null,\"thought_signature\":null,\"video_metadata\":null}],\"role\":\"model\"},\"grounding_metadata\":null,\"partial\":null,\"turn_complete\":null,\"finish_reason\":\"STOP\",\"error_code\":null,\"error_message\":null,\"interrupted\":null,\"custom_metadata\":{\"__llm_request_key__\":\"f3a0ac8c-3567-4867-9b8f-68f2a2a9f814\"},\"usage_metadata\":{\"cache_tokens_details\":null,\"cached_content_token_count\":null,\"candidates_token_count\":3065,\"candidates_tokens_details\":null,\"prompt_token_count\":1855,\"prompt_tokens_details\":[{\"modality\":\"TEXT\",\"token_count\":1855}]