es** | \u00a52,500 - \u00a54,000 | $17 - $27 | Based on 3-4 temple entries per day. |\\n| **Food & Drink** | \u00a56,000 - \u00a510,000 | $40 - $67 | Breakfast/Lunch/Dinner/Snacks. |\\n| **Local Transport** | \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | $7 - $10 | Daily bus/subway use (using IC Card). |\\n| **Cultural Activity** | Varies (Budgeted separately below) | - | Tea Ceremony, special workshops. |\\n| **TOTAL (Excl. Activity/Hotels)** | **\u00a59,500 - \u00a515,500** | **$64 - $104** | |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Detailed 7-Day Itinerary\\n\\n### Day 1: Arrival and The Spiritual Heart of Eastern Kyoto (Higashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Arrive in Kyoto, check into accommodation, purchase IC Card (e.g., ICOCA). | 3 hours | Quick breakfast near the station or hotel. | Familiarize yourself with the local bus network immediately. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Temple:** Explore the main hall and wooden veranda. | 2 hours | Lunch near Kiyomizu-dera (Try **Yuba**\u2014tofu skin specialties). | Be prepared for crowds; observe purification rituals at the Otowa Waterfall. |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| Walk the historic **Sannenzaka & Ninenzaka** streets. | 1.5 hours | Stop for Matcha soft serve or Japanese sweets. | Excellent for photography; watch for tourists photographing Maiko/Geiko respectfully. |\\n| **Evening** | Explore **Gion District** and **Hanamikoji Street**. | 2 hours | **Dinner:** Traditional *Kaiseki* (if budget allows) or affordable *Okonomiyaki* in the Pontocho Alley area. | Keep noise levels low in Gion; do not approach or stop Geiko/Maiko for photos. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kiyomizu-dera Entry: \u00a5400 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 2: Golden Beauty and Zen Contemplation (Northern Kyoto)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel North. **Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)**. | 1.5 hours | Grab a quick *Onigiri* (rice ball) for breakfast on the go. | Arrive right at opening (9:00 AM) to experience the reflection on the pond with fewer crowds. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ryoan-ji Temple:** Contemplate the famous rock garden. | 1.5 hours | Lunch near the temple area (simple noodle shop recommended). | Sit quietly and observe the rock garden; avoid touching the stones. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Nijo Castle:** Explore the former Shogun\u2019s residence, focusing on the Nightingale Floors. | 2 hours | Tea/Coffee break. | Listen for the squeaking \\\"nightingale floors\\\" designed as security alarms. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore Nishiki Market area for diverse street food or casual *Izakaya* dining. | Nishiki Market usually closes early, so focus on nearby restaurants. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Kinkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500, Ryoan-ji Entry: \u00a5600, Nijo Castle Entry: \u00a51,040 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 3: Immersion in Tradition \u2013 Tea Ceremony and Craft\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Cultural Workshop:** Participate in a formal **Tea Ceremony (Chanoyu)**. | 2 hours | Light breakfast before the ceremony. | **Crucial Cultural Deep Dive:** Book in advance. Focus on the precise movements and philosophy. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion):** Explore the dry sand garden (\\\"Sea of Silver Sand\\\"). | 1.5 hours | Lunch near Ginkaku-ji (Look for shops selling traditional Kyoto sweets). | A much calmer contrast to Kinkaku-ji; enjoy the moss garden paths. |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Philosopher\u2019s Path (Tetsugaku no Michi):** A reflective walk along the canal. | 1.5 hours | Stop at local craft shops along the path for browsing. | Especially beautiful during Sakura or autumn foliage season. |\\n| **Evening** | Free time/Relaxation. | Flexible | **Dinner:** Try Kyoto-style *Obanzai* (home-style local cooking). | Use this evening to relax after two intense days of sightseeing. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Ginkaku-ji Entry: \u00a5500. Tea Ceremony: \u00a53,000 - \u00a56,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 4: Thousand Gates and Southern Majesty (Fushimi & Nara Option)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Fushimi Inari Taisha Shrine:** Hike through the thousands of red *torii* gates. | 3 - 4 hours | Early light breakfast. Purchase local street snacks (like *Inari Sushi*) near the base. | Go as early as possible (before 8 AM) to experience the gates without massive crowds. You do not need to hike to the very top. |\\n| **Lunch** | Lunch near Fushimi Inari Station. | 1 hour | Lunch at a local spot specializing in eel or simple udon. | |\\n| **Afternoon (Option A)**| **Day Trip to Nara:** Visit Todai-ji Temple (Giant Buddha) and interact with the friendly wild deer. | 5 hours (Including travel) | Street snacks in Nara (e.g., *mochi* pounding demonstration). | Nara is 45 mins by JR train from Kyoto Station. |\\n| **Afternoon (Option B)**| **Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d:** Visit the hall containing 1,001 statues of Kannon. | 1.5 hours | Relaxing afternoon back in Kyoto. | Photography is strictly forbidden inside the hall; marvel at the precision of the statues. |\\n| **Evening** | **Dinner:** Explore dining options around Kyoto Station (great for variety and efficiency). | Flexible | Dinner: Ramen street in Kyoto Station or Isetan department store dining floors. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Fushimi Inari: Free. Sanj\u016bsangen-d\u014d: \u00a5600. Nara (Todai-ji): \u00a5600. | | | |\\n\\n### Day 5: Bamboo Groves and Western Serenity (Arashiyama)\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | Travel to Arashiyama (JR Saga Line recommended). **Arashiyama Bamboo Grove.** | 2 hours | Breakfast at the hotel or a bakery near Saga-Arashiyama station. | Visit the Bamboo Grove immediately upon arrival (before 9 AM) for the best experience. |\\n| **Late Morning**| **Tenry\u016b-ji Temple:** Explore the main temple and its extensive landscape garden. | 2 hours | Lunch near the Katsura River. Try vegetarian shojin ryori (monk's cuisine) if available. | A UNESCO site; the garden seamlessly integrates the surrounding mountains. |\\n| **Afternoon** | Cross the **Togetsukyo Bridge** and explore the smaller, quieter temples on the western bank (e.g., Okochi Sanso Villa). | 2.5 hours | Stop for sweets or green tea near the river. | Okochi Sanso (former actor's villa) offers spectacular views and includes tea in the entry fee. |\\n| **Evening** | Return to Central Kyoto. | 1 hour | **Dinner:** Explore the dining area near your hotel or try a specialized *Tempura* restaurant. | |\\n| **Budget Note** | Tenry\u016b-ji Entry: \u00a5500 (Garden only), Okochi Sanso: \u00a51,000 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 6: Deep Culture & Artistic Exploration\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Kyoto National Museum** or **Kyoto International Manga Museum** (depending on interest). | 3 hours | Breakfast/Brunch near the museum area. | Great for a slightly less temple-focused cultural day. |\\n| **Lunch** | Casual lunch. | 1 hour | Ramen or Curry. | |\\n| **Afternoon** | **Kiyomizu-dera Area Revisit (Optional):** Explore smaller, less-visited sub-temples (like K\u014ddai-ji) or traditional pottery shops in Kiyomizu-zaka. | 3 hours | Second chance for a traditional sweet treat. | This allows time for souvenir shopping focusing on local crafts (pottery, fans). |\\n| **Late Afternoon**| **Shinto Tradition Immersion:** Visit a smaller, local shrine (e.g., Kitano Tenman-g\u016b if time permits) to observe daily rituals. | 1.5 hours | | Observe the *ema* (prayer plaques) and local customs. |\\n| **Evening** | **Farewell Dinner:** Splurge on a memorable Kyoto dining experience (e.g., high-quality Wagyu or specialized Sushi). | Flexible | **Dinner:** Reservation highly recommended for the farewell meal. | Reflect on the trip; purchase any last-minute gifts. |\\n| **Budget Note** | Museum Entry: Approx. \u00a51,000 - \u00a51,500 | | | |\\n\\n### Day 7: Final Reflections and Departure\\n\\n| Time | Activity | Estimated Time | Meal/Dining Suggestion | Notes/Etiquette |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Morning** | **Morning Meditation/Temple Visit:** Revisit a favorite quiet spot (like a smaller Zen garden) or visit a local market for breakfast. | 2 hours | A final, classic Japanese breakfast set (grilled fish, rice, miso soup). | Use this time for quiet reflection before the busy travel day. |\\n| **Late Morning**| Final packing and check-out. | 1.5 hours | | Ensure all transportation tickets for onward travel are ready. |\\n| **Noon/Afternoon**| Travel to Kansai International Airport (KIX) or onward destination. | Varies | Quick and easy lunch near Kyoto Station before departure. | The JR Haruka Express is the most efficient way to KIX from Kyoto Station (approx. 75 minutes). |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Major Budget Estimates (7 Days, Per Person)\\n\\n| Expense Category | Low Estimate (USD) | Mid-Range Estimate (USD) | Notes |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Accommodation (7 Nights)** | $700 (Budget Hostel/Guesthouse) | $1,200 (Mid-range Hotel/Airbnb) | Highly variable based on season. |\\n| **Food & Dining (7 Days)** | $300 ($43/day) | $550 ($78/day) | Includes one minor splurge meal. |\\n| **Activities/Entry Fees** | $100 | $150 | Covers all temple/museum entries researched above. |\\n| **Cultural Experiences (Tea Ceremony)** | $40 | $60 | |\\n| **Local Transport (IC Card Top-ups)** | $50 | $70 | Covers local travel within Kyoto city limits. |\\n| **Intercity Travel (e.g., to KIX)** | $30 | $40 | One-way estimate to major airport/station. |\\n| **TOTAL ESTIMATE (Excluding Flights)** | **$1,220** | **$2,070** | |\"},\"artifact_delta\":{},\"transfer_to_agent\":null,\"escalate\":null,\"requested_auth_configs\":{},\"requested_tool_confirmations\":{},\"compaction\":null,\"end_of_agent\":null,\"agent_state\":null,\"rewind_before_invocation_id\":null},\"long_running_tool_ids\":null,\"branch\":null,\"id\":\"b425e697-33c8-4bd0-9b46-98f11629d1e7\",\"timestamp\":1763640874.075096},{\"model_version\":\"gemini-2.5-flash-lite-preview-09-2025\",\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"function_call\":null,\"code_execution_result\":null,\"executable_code\":null,\"file_data\":null,\"function_response\":null,\"inline_data\":null,\"text\":\"ITINERARY: ItineraryBuilderAgent has processed the destination research and formulated a comprehensive 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive Itinerary for Kyoto.\\n\\nThis itinerary balances iconic sightseeing with immersive cultural activities, ensuring efficient travel by grouping attractions geographically.\\n\\n---\\n\\n# 7-Day Cultural Deep-Dive: Kyoto Immersion\\n\\n**Focus:** Temples, Traditions, Tea Ceremonies, and Nature\\n**Recommended Travel Season:** Spring (April/May) or Autumn (October/November)\\n\\n## Suggested Accommodation Areas\\n\\nFor the best balance of accessibility, atmosphere, and proximity to dining:\\n\\n1.  **Higashiyama/Gion Area (Recommended for Culture):** Closest to many iconic temples, traditional streets, and evening atmosphere. Excellent for soaking in the old-Kyoto vibe.\\n2.  **Kawaramachi/Downtown Area:** Central hub, best for transit links (buses/subway) and modern dining variety.\\n3.  **Central Kyoto (Near Kyoto Station):** Best for day trips and ease of arrival/departure, but slightly less charming for evening ambiance.\\n\\n## Estimated Daily Budget Breakdown (Excluding Accommodation)\\n\\nThis is a mid-range estimate for one person, assuming IC card usage for transit and a mix of casual and slightly nicer dinners.\\n\\n| Expense Category | Estimated 